00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.000
We had crossed the French border. 

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.000
We knew the end of our journey was near, so we made it last. 

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:19.000
We were only flying a few hours a night, then spent the rest of the time eating and resting in pine forests. 

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:30.000
During the day, the woods were quiet: wild animals were hiding in their dens, away from the heat, while the sound of cicadas invited us to nap. 

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
It had been a beautiful day. Banjoko and I had stopped by a river to drink, then dozed off in a large pine tree. 

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Human voices echoed in the distance, but they were too far away to worry us. 

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:50.000
The heat enhanced the forest scents, the smell of thyme, wormwood and pine needles baked by the sun. 

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Then, I noticed another smell. Very faint at first, but unmistakable. Banjoko noticed it too. Smoke. 

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.000
This instantly shook us out of our slumber. 

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Around us, I could hear the forest waking up in panic. Birds calling, crickets shrieking, foxes and wild boars stampeding across the woods.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.000
And in the distance, the crackling, snapping, popping of burning branches.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Banjoko and I took off in a frenzy. But where to go? 

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
A blanket of smoke covered everything, clouds of black fumes billowed into the sky. 

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:41.000
I was flying in a dense fog of panic, helplessly flapping my wings, unsure about what to do.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:47.000
“The river!” I heard Banjoko’s voice behind me. “Bird, can you find it?”

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
I looked for my friend, but I couldn’t see him. 

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.000
“Follow me”, I shouted. And I plunged towards the ground. 

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Travelling at lower altitude was unbearable. 

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:06.000
The heat was scorching, the acrid smell of charred wood filled the air. But there was no other way. 

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:16.000
The flames raced along, twigs, branches and trunks turning into ash all around us. We could hear the pitiful cries of creatures trapped in their burrows. 

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.000
And then, I saw it ahead of me. The river. 

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.000
I raced towards it and crashed on the riverbed. 

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.000
My heart was racing, my lungs were scorched, but I could finally breathe.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Above me, the sky was ablaze with colour. I watched in horror as the forest disintegrated before me. 

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.000
And then I remembered my friend. 

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.000
“Banjoko!” I cried.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.000
But he didn’t answer me. 

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.000
I was alone. 
